§ `Chapters 20 to 22 -- amercements`

§§ `Chapter 20 -- freemen, merchants and villeins`

-- 20.2 saves a freeman's contenement; 20.3 saves a merchant's wares; 20.4 saves a villein's wainage.
-- The saving is read as holding for any amercement of that person, small or grave (fork F20b).
-- gloss (not the text's): contenement, wares and wainage are what keeps each of them in his
-- livelihood: a freeman's holding, a merchant's stock, a villein's implements of husbandry.
-- src:47 | and for a grave offence he shall be amerced in accordance with the gravity of his offence, yet saving always his “contenement”;
-- src:47 | and a merchant in the same way, saving his wares;
-- src:47 | and a villein shall be amerced in the same way, saving his wainage—if they have fallen into our mercy:
@ref Chapter 20 clause 20.2-20.4 (src:47)
GIVEN class IS A ClassOfPerson
GIVETH A SavedItem
DECIDE `ch 20 -- what is saved to a person of this class when he is amerced` IS
    CONSIDER class
    WHEN `a freeman` THEN `his contenement`
    WHEN `a merchant` THEN `his wares`
    WHEN `a villein` THEN `his wainage`

-- 20.1 and 20.2: the amercement is in accordance with the degree or gravity of the offence.
-- 20.5: "none of the aforesaid amercements shall be imposed except by the oath of honest men of the
-- neighbourhood".  The conditions of 20.4, "if they have fallen into our mercy", are the first fact.
-- src:47 | A freeman shall not be amerced for a small offence, except in accordance with the degree of the offence;
-- src:47 | and for a grave offence he shall be amerced in accordance with the gravity of his offence, yet saving always his “contenement”;
-- src:47 | and a merchant in the same way, saving his wares;
-- src:47 | and a villein shall be amerced in the same way, saving his wainage—if they have fallen into our mercy:
-- src:47 | and none of the aforesaid amercements shall be imposed except by the oath of honest men of the neighbourhood.
@ref Chapter 20 clause 20.1-20.5 (src:47)
GIVEN `he has fallen into the King's mercy` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the amercement is in accordance with the degree or gravity of the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the amercement leaves him what is saved to him` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is imposed by the oath of honest men of the neighbourhood` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Bar
DECIDE `ch 20 -- the amercement may be imposed` IS
    IF `he has fallen into the King's mercy`
    THEN IF   `the amercement is in accordance with the degree or gravity of the offence`
          AND `the amercement leaves him what is saved to him`
          AND `it is imposed by the oath of honest men of the neighbourhood`
         -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "except in accordance with the degree of the offence", "saving" what is saved, "if they have fallen into our mercy" and "except by the oath of honest men of the neighbourhood".
         THEN `Not barred`
         ELSE Barred
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if they have fallen into our mercy".
    ELSE `Not barred`

§§ `Chapter 21 -- earls and barons`

-- src:49 | Earls and barons shall not be amerced except through their peers, and only in accordance with the degree of the offence.
@ref Chapter 21 (src:49)
GIVEN `he is an earl or a baron` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the amercement is through his peers` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the amercement is in accordance with the degree of the offence` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Bar
DECIDE `ch 21 -- the earl or baron may be amerced in this way` IS
    IF `he is an earl or a baron`
    THEN IF   `the amercement is through his peers`
          AND `the amercement is in accordance with the degree of the offence`
         -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "except through their peers, and only in accordance with the degree of the offence".
         THEN `Not barred`
         ELSE Barred
    -- NOTHING-kind (word not the condition here: except, only because "except" and "only" qualify how an earl or baron may be amerced; the branch is reached by a person who is not an earl or baron).
    ELSE `Bar not covered`

§§ `Chapter 22 -- clerks`

-- "after the manner of the others aforesaid" is read as the manner of chapter 20 and chapter 21; a
-- clerk's own class is not stated, so whether the manner was followed is a plain fact (fork F22).
-- src:51 | A clerk shall not be amerced in respect of his lay holding except after the manner of the others aforesaid;
-- src:51 | further, he shall not be amerced in accordance with the extent of his church benefice.
@ref Chapter 22 (src:51)
GIVEN `he is a clerk` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the amercement is in respect of his lay holding` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the amercement is after the manner of the others aforesaid` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the amercement is in accordance with the extent of his church benefice` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A Bar
DECIDE `ch 22 -- the clerk may be amerced in this way` IS
    IF `he is a clerk`
    THEN IF `the amercement is in accordance with the extent of his church benefice`
         THEN Barred
         ELSE IF `the amercement is in respect of his lay holding`
              THEN IF `the amercement is after the manner of the others aforesaid`
                   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "except after the manner of the others aforesaid".
                   THEN `Not barred`
                   ELSE Barred
              -- NOTHING-kind (word not the condition here: except because "except" carves out the manner of an amercement of the lay holding; this amercement is neither of the lay holding nor by the benefice, so the exception is not reached and the text is silent).
              ELSE `Bar not covered`
    -- NOTHING-kind (word not the condition here: except because "except" qualifies how a clerk may be amerced; the branch is reached by a person who is not a clerk).
    ELSE `Bar not covered`
